From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:20:09 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] i386-asm: print constant asm operands as 64-bit values

A constant "i"/"n" operand was printed with "%d" of an int, truncating
64-bit constants.  The Linux kernel's runtime_const_ptr() emits
"mov %1,%0" with "i"(0x0123456789abcdefull) and later patches the 8
immediate bytes; tcc produced a 7-byte "mov $-1985229329,%rax" and the
patch clobbered the following instruction.
---
 i386-asm.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/i386-asm.c b/i386-asm.c
index 76a2498..7d6d3b7 100644
--- a/i386-asm.c
+++ b/i386-asm.c
@@ -1532,7 +1532,8 @@ ST_FUNC void asm_compute_constraints(ASMOperand *operands,
 ST_FUNC void subst_asm_operand(CString *add_str,
                               SValue *sv, int modifier)
 {
-    int r, reg, size, val;
+    int r, reg, size;
+    long long val;
 
     r = sv->r;
     if ((r & VT_VALMASK) == VT_CONST) {
@@ -1556,10 +1557,13 @@ ST_FUNC void subst_asm_operand(CString *add_str,
                 goto no_offset;
 	    cstr_ccat(add_str, '+');
         }
+        /* a 64-bit constant must not be truncated: the kernel's
+           runtime_const_ptr() places `mov $0x0123456789abcdef, %reg`
+           and patches the 8 immediate bytes later */
         val = sv->c.i;
         if (modifier == 'n')
             val = -val;
-        cstr_printf(add_str, "%d", val);
+        cstr_printf(add_str, "%lld", val);
     no_offset:;
 #ifdef TCC_TARGET_X86_64
         if (r & VT_LVAL)
